    "updatedAt": "2026-10-22T01:48:18.639Z",
    "reason": "This PR's wrong transformation (using an ordinary RawPtrKind::Const deep shared borrow instead of the shallow FakeForPtrMetadata borrow, and/or omitting the array FakeRead) is a borrowck/CTFE-retagging regression, not a value-semantics bug. The actual regression test (src/tools/miri/tests/pass/disjoint-array-accesses.rs, revisions stack/tree) is a pass test that checks disjoint mutable borrows of array/slice elements and fields compile; with the wrong lowering the program is rejected by borrowck, and under CTFE/Miri retagging the failure mode is UB. The corrected fake borrow is operationally equivalent to the old Rvalue::Len, and FakeRead is pure borrowck metadata, so both correct and incorrect lowerings compute the same length/value at runtime. There is no input on which both functions (a) compile, (b) pass Miri as defined executions, and (c) produce different values; producing one would require injecting UB/compile failure or an unrelated value change, which is not the historical transformation.",
    "evidence": [],
    "attempts": 0
  },
  "analysis": {
    "kind": "bug-fix",
    "summary": "Re-land lowering of index bounds checks from `Rvalue::Len` to array constants plus `PtrMetadata`, while restoring the shallow-fake-borrow semantics that `Len` had. Arrays use `FakeRead(ForIndex, place)` plus `const N`; slices use `PtrMetadata` on either the copied slice local or a new `RawPtrKind::FakeForPtrMetadata` raw pointer. This fake raw pointer is shallow over `ArtificialField::ArrayLength` for borrowck and is never retagged in CTFE/Miri, avoiding the disjoint-borrow regression from issue #135671. Downstream changes: InstSimplify no longer needs `Len([_; N]) -> N`; GVN must key raw-address values by `RawPtrKind` and can deduplicate `PtrMetadata` length loads; Validator permits `PtrMetadata` in non-runtime MIR because it is now introduced during MIR building.",
    "originalLogic": "Before this PR, indexing a slice or array emitted `Rvalue::Len(place)` for the bounds-check length. `Len` behaved as a shallow fake borrow of the array/slice length (`ArtificialField::ArrayLength`), which allowed borrowck to accept disjoint borrows of different array elements or fields after the bounds check. Array lengths could later be simplified by InstSimplify from `Len([_; N])` to the constant `N`. The PR instead computes the length at MIR building: for `ty::Array(_, len)` it emits `FakeRead(ForIndex, place)` and uses `Operand::Constant` for `len`; for `ty::Slice(_)` it emits `Rvalue::UnaryOp(UnOp::PtrMetadata, ptr_or_ref)`, where `ptr_or_ref` is either a direct copy of a trivially-copy slice local/place or the result of `Rvalue::RawPtr(RawPtrKind::FakeForPtrMetadata, place)`.",
    "incorrectTransformation": "The historically incorrect transformation, identified by the PR title/description and regression test, is lowering the bounds-check length to `PtrMetadata` using an ordinary raw/shared borrow instead of the `Len`-equivalent shallow fake borrow, and/or not preserving the array use via a fake read. A normal `RawPtrKind::Const` borrow is a deep shared raw borrow, is retagged in CTFE, and conflicts with mutable borrows of disjoint elements/fields; the corrected transformation uses `RawPtrKind::FakeForPtrMetadata`, which borrowck treats as `Shallow(Some(ArtificialField::ArrayLength)), Read(ReadKind::Copy)` and which `is_fake()` excludes from retagging. The regression test states that a prior MIR refactor about arrays and their lengths \"unexpectedly caused borrowck errors for disjoint borrows of array elements\".",
    "inputDomain": "Rust code indexing arrays `[T; N]`, const-generic arrays `[T; N]`, shared slices `&[T]`, mutable slices `&mut [T]`, and custom dynamically-sized types with a slice tail; indexing behind references, `Box<[T]>`, or projections such as `(*_1).1`; repeated indexing of the same slice; disjoint mutable borrows of array elements or struct fields; panic=abort and panic=unwind; 32-bit and 64-bit targets; MIR phases from built through runtime optimization.",
    "passIds": [
      "InstSimplify",
      "GVN",
      "Validator"
    ],
    "trigger": "A MIR producer or rewrite replaces `Rvalue::Len` on arrays/slices with a length computation for a bounds check. For slices, the new computation must obtain metadata through a shallow fake raw borrow; for arrays, it must emit `FakeRead(ForIndex, place)` and use `const N`. Trigger tests with multiple indices into one slice or mutable disjoint element/field borrows after an index.",
    "preconditions": [
      "The place is an array or slice.",
      "For a slice fast path `Operand::Copy(Place::from(place.local))` is only valid when the projection is exactly `[PlaceElem::Deref]` and the local type is `is_trivially_pure_clone_copy()`; otherwise a `RawPtrKind::FakeForPtrMetadata` temporary is required.",
      "The fake raw borrow is only used to access metadata, is treated as shallow with `ArtificialField::ArrayLength`, and must not be retagged.",
      "Array bounds checks still need a fake read even though the length is a compile-time constant."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
      "Do not model the fake metadata borrow as a normal `*const`/shared deep borrow: that reintroduces borrowck conflicts and CTFE retagging.",
      "Do not merge `RawPtrKind::FakeForPtrMetadata` with `RawPtrKind::Const` in GVN value numbering merely because `to_mutbl_lossy()` maps both to `*const`; the PR changes `AddressKind::Address` to carry `RawPtrKind`.",
      "Do not `copy` a `&mut [T]` local for the built MIR fast path; the test comments explicitly say the built MIR cannot do this and needs the fake raw pointer, while runtime MIR may differ.",
      "Do not omit the array `FakeRead(ForIndex, ...)`, since it preserves borrow/initialization use tracking.",
      "Do not assume the validator enforces that the fake pointer is used only for metadata; the `RawPtrKind` docs say that limitation is not enforced by the validator.",
      "Do not keep an InstSimplify `Len([_; N]) -> N` rule as the only array-length optimization; the new bounds-check MIR may contain no `Len` at all."
    ],
    "testStrategy": "Add MIR-building FileCheck tests for arrays, const-generic arrays, slices, mutable slices, and custom slice-tail DSTs. Assert array MIR has `FakeRead(ForIndex, ...)` and `Lt(..., const N)` with no `Len`; assert slice MIR has `PtrMetadata(copy _1)` for copy slice locals and `&raw const (fake) ...` followed by `PtrMetadata` for mutable/custom places. Check `&mut [i32]` built MIR does not simply copy the mutable reference. Add a GVN test with three bounds checks on one slice asserting one `PtrMetadata` load followed by three `Lt`/`assert` uses and `CHECK-NOT: PtrMetadata` between them. Add a Miri pass regression test for disjoint mutable borrows of array/slice elements and fields, under both stacked and tree borrows. Exercise both panic strategies and bit widths. Verify Validator accepts `PtrMetadata` outside runtime MIR and InstSimplify no longer relies on `Len` simplification.",
    "evidence": [
      "Then introduce `RawRefKind::FakeForPtrMetadata` and use that for lowering index bounds checking to a `PtrMetadata`.",
      "This new `RawRefKind::FakeForPtrMetadata` acts like a shallow fake borrow in borrowck, which mimics the semantics of the old `Rvalue::Len` operation we're replacing.",
      "RawPtrKind::FakeForPtrMetadata => {",
      "(Shallow(Some(ArtificialField::ArrayLength)), Read(ReadKind::Copy))",
      "The borrow checker allows overlap of these raw pointers with references to the",
      "data. This is sound even if the pointer is \"misused\" since any such use is anyway",
      "unsafe. In terms of the operational semantics (i.e., Miri), this is equivalent",
